WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:02.571
Lesson 8, 90 minutes.

00:00:03.026 --> 00:00:05.270
Combining tree-ring evidence.

00:00:05.724 --> 00:00:09.454
The final lesson turns the methods into a research plan.

00:00:09.908 --> 00:00:15.487
Measuring rings, assigning dates, interpreting environmental conditions and explaining human

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:18.059
activities are connected tasks.

00:00:18.513 --> 00:00:21.803
Each connection needs its own supporting evidence.

00:00:22.263 --> 00:00:26.947
We will practise making those steps explicit rather than jumping from a date to a complete

00:00:26.961 --> 00:00:28.941
historical explanation.

00:00:29.395 --> 00:00:34.184
This video contains the explanations for a ninety-minute classroom lesson.

00:00:34.645 --> 00:00:37.680
Pause to complete the exercises.

00:00:38.483 --> 00:00:42.372
Separate evidence for dates, environment and causes.

00:00:42.827 --> 00:00:46.321
Interpret agreement and disagreement among sources.

00:00:46.775 --> 00:00:50.255
Develop a testable question and an evidence plan.

00:00:50.709 --> 00:00:52.834
There are two exercises.

00:00:53.288 --> 00:00:58.031
First we interpret several dating clues without claiming more than they support.

00:00:58.485 --> 00:01:03.419
Then we turn a personal area of interest into a plan with a defined target, suitable

00:01:03.433 --> 00:01:09.340
materials, an analysis, and alternative explanations that can be tested.

00:01:10.143 --> 00:01:14.822
Archaeology asks about objects, buildings and human activity.

00:01:15.277 --> 00:01:18.849
Ecology asks about changes in trees and stands.

00:01:19.303 --> 00:01:23.626
Climatology asks about defined regional and seasonal climate.

00:01:24.079 --> 00:01:29.698
The applications share recording, dating, comparisons and explicit limits, but they ask

00:01:29.711 --> 00:01:31.323
different questions.

00:01:31.777 --> 00:01:37.145
Strong evidence for an object's age is not automatically evidence for a climate event or a

00:01:37.158 --> 00:01:38.599
social cause.

00:01:39.053 --> 00:01:44.552
Identify what each observation supports before combining it with another field.

00:01:45.355 --> 00:01:49.343
Observation: directly measured or inspected features.

00:01:49.805 --> 00:01:53.706
Dating: placement by crossdating and calendar anchoring.

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:59.338
Interpretation: inference about cutting, disturbance, climate or activity.

00:01:59.791 --> 00:02:02.502
Bark preservation is an observation.

00:02:02.963 --> 00:02:06.640
Assigning the adjacent ring to a year is a dating result.

00:02:07.094 --> 00:02:13.120
Inferring felling or construction adds assumptions about preservation, processing and use.

00:02:13.581 --> 00:02:19.188
Writing these as separate statements is a practical way to detect gaps in the reasoning.

00:02:19.991 --> 00:02:24.545
Ring formation, felling, use and deposition are different events.

00:02:25.006 --> 00:02:28.684
Reuse and repair can separate timber and building dates.

00:02:29.138 --> 00:02:32.026
Check the evidence connecting these stages.

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:36.987
This diagram shows stages rather than dates from a particular site.

00:02:37.441 --> 00:02:42.414
A lower bound for one stage does not specify the length of the next interval.

00:02:42.875 --> 00:02:48.309
Avoid silently assuming that felling, manufacture, use and deposition happened at the same

00:02:48.322 --> 00:02:49.744
time.

00:02:50.547 --> 00:02:54.515
Coinciding changes can motivate a causal hypothesis.

00:02:54.970 --> 00:03:01.003
Examine food, water, land use, institutions and other mediating processes.

00:03:01.457 --> 00:03:05.049
Match region, season and temporal resolution.

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:10.687
A cold period and a social change can coincide without establishing a cause.

00:03:11.141 --> 00:03:16.155
Investigate the processes that might connect them, including which season matters to crops

00:03:16.168 --> 00:03:17.720
or water supply.

00:03:18.181 --> 00:03:23.773
Check that the climatic and historical sources refer to comparable places, seasons and time

00:03:23.786 --> 00:03:25.484
resolution.
